      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Nestled on Nottawasaga Bay, Collingwood’s waterfront once shipped grain to Chicago and Thunder Bay via its rail‑connected harbour.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collingwood%2C_Ontario",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Collingwood launched Canada’s first steel‑hulled ship, the Huronic, in 1901, and later built corvettes for the Royal Canadian Navy during WWII.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collingwood%2C_Ontario",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Collingwood Museum, housed in an 1873 train station, displays the town’s shipbuilding history and other local artifacts.",
